  },
  {
    "id": "little-giant-vcma-15uls",
    "name": "Little Giant VCMA-15ULS Condensate Pump with Safety Switch",
    "category": "Condensate Pump, 65 GPH at 1 ft, 15 ft Shutoff Head, Overflow Switch",
    "badge": "Best Condensate Pump",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Built-in safety switch can shut off the dehumidifier if the pump fails",
      "15-foot shutoff head, enough to lift water out of most attics to a drain",
      "Half-gallon reservoir, removable check valve and 3/8-inch barbed outlet",
      "Small 1/50 HP motor on a standard 115-volt outlet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Flow drops sharply with height, to about 25 GPH at 10 feet on Little Giant's figures",
      "Tubing is not included, and the safety switch must be wired to work"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The pump to add to any attic dehumidifier without one, mainly for the overflow safety switch that protects the ceiling below.",
    "description": "Every attic dehumidifier without a built-in pump needs a way to move its water, and the Little Giant VCMA-15ULS is the condensate pump HVAC contractors install under furnaces and air handlers every day. It is a 1/50 horsepower, 115-volt pump with a half-gallon reservoir, rated at 65 gallons per hour at one foot of lift and a 15-foot shutoff head; Little Giant's catalog gives about 50 gallons per hour at 5 feet and 25 at 10 feet for the closely related model, which is still far more than any dehumidifier here produces. The outlet is a 3/8-inch barbed fitting with a removable check valve. The feature that justifies it in an attic is the S in the model number: a built-in safety switch that opens if the water level rises too high, which can be wired to cut power to the dehumidifier so a failed pump does not mean water through the ceiling. That wiring is a job for an electrician or HVAC installer unless you are confident with low-voltage and line-voltage controls. It is the one budget item in this guide and usually sells for well under $100. Pair it with a drain pan under the dehumidifier."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Attic Dehumidifier",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-attic-dehumidifier/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a dehumidifier in my attic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not, if the attic is vented. A vented attic's moisture problems come from air leaks, bathroom fans venting into the attic or blocked soffits, and fixing those is the answer. A sealed or spray foam attic is different: if a hygrometer shows humidity staying above about 60% in summer, a compact dehumidifier or HVAC supply air is often needed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best dehumidifier for an attic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a sealed attic, the Santa Fe Compact70 is our top pick for its small, hangable cabinet, duct kits and published 70°F output. Choose the AlorAir HD55P if you have no downhill drain route, the HD35P for a small attic, and the AprilAire E080 if you want to duct it into an air handler in the attic."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my spray foam attic so humid?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A sealed attic is part of the house but often gets no conditioned air. Moisture from the living space, leaky ducts and vapor moving through open-cell foam builds up with nowhere to go. Building Science Corporation recommends either supply air at about 50 CFM per 1,000 square feet of attic or dehumidification to keep it in check."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What humidity should a sealed attic be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Aim to keep a sealed attic at or below about 60% relative humidity, and ideally close to the 45% to 55% you keep in the living space. Readings consistently above 60%, condensation on ducts or a musty smell mean the attic needs supply air, dehumidification or a search for air leaks to the outside."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I put a portable dehumidifier in my attic?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A standard portable is a poor choice. Its bucket fills quickly, most have no pump, few can be ducted or hung, and none is designed to run unattended above a finished ceiling. Purpose-built crawl space and whole-house units are designed for unattended operation, ducting, continuous drainage and the higher temperatures a sealed attic can reach."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Where does the water go from an attic dehumidifier?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Into a drain line, either by gravity to an existing condensate line or drain, or by pump to a drain, sink or exterior outlet. Units like the AlorAir HD55P include a pump; others need an external condensate pump. Always add a secondary drain pan with a float switch under the unit to protect the ceiling below."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will an attic fan reduce attic humidity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "In a vented attic, an attic fan moves air but can make moisture worse by depressurizing the attic and pulling humid, conditioned air up through ceiling leaks. Air sealing and passive soffit and ridge vents are usually more effective. In a sealed attic, an attic fan should never be used, because it breaks the seal the design depends on."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Basement Waterproofing",
    tags: [
      "attic dehumidifier",
      "attic dehumidifiers",
      "best dehumidifier for attic",
      "best attic dehumidifier",
      "dehumidifier for spray foam attic",
      "encapsulated attic dehumidifier",
      "conditioned attic humidity"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Attic Dehumidifiers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Six compact, ductable dehumidifiers for sealed and spray foam attics, and an honest look at why most vented attics need air sealing and ventilation, not a dehumidifier.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#santa-fe-compact70-crawl-space"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Do You Need One?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#need-one" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Do You Need One?</a>
          <a href="#ventilation-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Fix Vented Attics First</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The 6 Best, Ranked</a>
          <a href="#sealed-attics" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Spray Foam and Sealed Attics</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Sizing</a>
          <a href="#heat-tolerance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Heat Tolerance</a>
          <a href="#condensate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Condensate and Pumps</a>
          <a href="#installation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Hanging and Ducting</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most attics do not need a dehumidifier. A normal vented attic with damp sheathing, frost on the nails or a musty smell has a moisture source or a ventilation problem, and a dehumidifier up there would be trying to dry the outdoors. The attic that genuinely needs one is the sealed kind: a conditioned or encapsulated attic with spray foam under the roof deck, where humid air has nowhere to go and humidity can climb past 60% every summer. This guide explains how to tell which attic you have, what to fix first, and which six compact, hangable, ductable units suit a sealed attic.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="need-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Does Your Attic Actually Need a Dehumidifier?
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vented vs Sealed Attics</h3>

          <p>
            Start with what kind of attic you have, because the answer is completely different for the two types. A vented attic has insulation on the attic floor, vents at the soffits and ridge or gables, and air that is meant to be close to outdoor conditions. A sealed attic, also called conditioned, unvented or encapsulated, has insulation on the underside of the roof deck, usually spray foam, no vents to the outside, and air that is meant to behave like the rest of the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier in a vented attic is almost always the wrong answer. That space exchanges air with the outdoors all day, so a dehumidifier would be trying to dry the weather. In summer the attic can exceed the heat rating of every unit in this guide, and in winter the moisture problem is usually frost from warm, damp house air leaking up through the ceiling, which no dehumidifier in the attic will stop. The fix for a vented attic is to stop the moisture getting in and let the vents carry away the rest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Sealed Attics Get Humid</h3>

          <p>
            A sealed attic is different. Once the roof deck is insulated and the vents are closed, the attic becomes part of the house, but it often gets none of the air conditioning. Moisture that diffuses through open-cell foam, rises from the living space or leaks from ductwork has nowhere to go. Owners of spray foam attics in humid climates commonly report attic humidity of 60% to 70% in summer while the rooms below sit near 50%. That is the attic a dehumidifier is designed for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Hygrometer Test</h3>

          <p>
            The quickest test is a cheap digital hygrometer left in the attic for a week in summer. In a sealed attic, readings that stay above about 60% relative humidity, a musty smell or condensation on ducts mean you need either supply air or dehumidification. In a vented attic, readings that track outdoor humidity are normal, and wet sheathing, mold on the north side of the roof or frost in winter point to air leaks and ventilation, which the next section covers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HONEST ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Vented attic: fix the source. Sealed attic: dehumidify.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your attic has soffit and ridge vents and floor insulation, a dehumidifier is not the solution. If it has spray foam on the roof deck and no vents, a compact, ducted dehumidifier, or supply air from the HVAC system, is often exactly what it needs.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="ventilation-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Vented Attics: Fix Ventilation and Air Leaks First
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Seal Air Leaks From the House Below</h3>

          <p>
            Most moisture in a vented attic comes from the house below, not the outdoors. Warm, humid indoor air rises and leaks through gaps around ceiling light fixtures, plumbing and wiring penetrations, top plates, the attic hatch and chimney chases. When it reaches cold roof sheathing in winter it condenses or frosts, and in spring the frost melts and drips. Air sealing those leaks from the attic side, with foam, caulk and a weatherstripped, insulated hatch cover, is the single most effective attic moisture fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vent Every Exhaust Outside</h3>

          <p>
            Next, check that bathroom, kitchen and dryer exhausts actually reach the outside. A bath fan that dumps into the attic, or whose flex duct has come loose and lies on the insulation, can put a surprising amount of water into the attic every week. Each exhaust should run through an insulated duct to a roof cap, wall cap or soffit vent fitted with a damper, never just near a soffit vent where the moist air can be drawn straight back in.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear the Soffits and Fix the Vents</h3>

          <p>
            Then look at the ventilation itself. Insulation blown over the soffits is common and blocks the intake vents; rafter baffles restore the airflow. Mixed vent types, such as a ridge vent combined with gable vents or a powered fan, can short-circuit each other. A powered attic fan can make moisture worse by depressurizing the attic and pulling conditioned, humid air up from the house. Our guide to the <a href="/best-attic-fan/">best attic fans</a> explains when one helps and when it does more harm than good.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Then Judge What Remains</h3>

          <p>
            Only when the leaks are sealed, the exhausts are vented outside and the soffits are clear should you judge whether a moisture problem remains. In a vented attic, the answer is almost always no. If it is still damp, the next step is usually more intake ventilation or a roof or flashing leak, not a dehumidifier, and in some cases converting the attic to a sealed design, which is a job for an insulation contractor rather than an appliance purchase.
          </p>
        </div>

