        <div id="sealed-attics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Spray Foam and Sealed Attics: Where a Dehumidifier Belongs
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Sealed Attics Trap Moisture</h3>

          <p>
            Sealed attics became common because they bring ductwork and air handlers inside the thermal envelope, which saves energy. The catch is that the attic is now a large, enclosed volume of indoor air that often has no supply or return register. Building Science Corporation, one of the most cited sources on the subject, lists two remedies for conditioned attics: supply air from the HVAC system at about 50 CFM per 1,000 square feet of attic, or dehumidification.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Open-Cell Foam Factor</h3>

          <p>
            Open-cell spray foam makes the problem more likely. It is vapor-permeable, so in a humid climate some moisture moves through it, and it is air-permeable, which is why recent editions of the International Residential Code, section R806.5, tie a supply air requirement to certain unvented attics insulated with air-permeable insulation directly under the roof deck. Closed-cell foam resists vapor much more, but a sealed attic with closed-cell foam can still collect humidity from the house and from leaky ducts. Check the code your jurisdiction has adopted before you design around it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a Dehumidifier Is the Right Tool</h3>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier is the right tool when supply air alone does not hold the attic below about 60% humidity, when the HVAC system runs too little in spring and fall to dry the attic, or when adding a supply register is impractical. It is also the right tool when the attic contains a finished room or storage you want to protect. In each case, the dehumidifier should be sized for a modest load, because a properly sealed attic gains moisture slowly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think of the attic dehumidifier as a crawl space dehumidifier turned upside down. The space is sealed, the moisture load is modest and steady, access is awkward, and the machine needs to run for years with little attention. It is no coincidence that most of the picks here are crawl space units; our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">crawl space dehumidifier guide</a> covers the same machines from the other end of the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sizing an Attic Dehumidifier (Smaller Than You Think)
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Smaller Attics Need Smaller Units</h3>

          <p>
            A sealed attic should need far less dehumidifying than a basement. There is no soil or foundation wall adding moisture, no laundry and no bathrooms. The load comes from vapor diffusing through the foam, air leaking from the house and from ducts, and any remaining gaps at the eaves. For most attics up to about 1,000 square feet, a unit rated at 35 pints per day at AHAM conditions is enough, which is why the AlorAir HD35P is on this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When to Step Up to 55 to 70 Pints</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to a 55 to 70 pint unit for attics of roughly 1,000 to 2,000 square feet, for open-cell foam in a hot, humid climate, or where the attic contains the air handler and long duct runs. The Santa Fe Compact70, AlorAir HD55P and AprilAire E070 sit in that band. The AprilAire E080, at 80 pints, makes sense when you are ducting it into the HVAC system to treat the attic and part of the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Lower-Temperature Ratings</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention to the lower-temperature ratings, not just the headline. Santa Fe publishes 47 pints at 70°F for the Compact70, and AprilAire publishes 53 pints at 73°F for the E070 and 65 pints at 73°F for the E080. A sealed attic tied into an air-conditioned house often sits in the 70s, so those are the figures that describe real output. The 80°F AHAM rating will overstate what you get on a mild day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Cost of Oversizing</h3>

          <p>
            Oversizing has costs. A big unit in a small, tight attic short-cycles, running for a few minutes and shutting off before it has removed much water, which wastes energy and wears the compressor. Undersizing is the more forgiving mistake in a sealed attic, because the load is steady, but if a smaller unit runs continuously through the humid season and the hygrometer still reads above 60%, look for an air leak before buying a bigger machine. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a> guide covers sizing for the rooms below.
          </p>
        </div>

