            6. Heat Tolerance: Why the Spec Sheet's Maximum Matters Up There
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Maximum Operating Temperatures</h3>

          <p>
            Every compressor dehumidifier has a maximum operating temperature, and in an attic it is the specification that matters most. The Santa Fe Compact70 is listed at 95°F maximum. AprilAire gives a dehumidification inlet range of 50°F to 104°F for both the E070 and E080. AlorAir lists 33°F to 105°F for the HD55P and 33.8°F to 110°F for the HD35P. Above those limits, compressor pressures climb, efficiency falls and the unit may shut down on its own protection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Vented vs Sealed Attic Temperatures</h3>

          <p>
            A vented attic in summer can easily run well above all of those figures, which is one more reason not to put a dehumidifier there. A properly sealed and foamed attic is a different environment: because the roof deck is insulated, the attic usually stays within a modest range of the house temperature. A hygrometer that also logs temperature is worth leaving up there for a few hot days before you buy, so you know which machines your attic actually suits.
          </p>

