        <div id="condensate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Condensate: Pumps, Pans and Where the Water Goes
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Drainage Can Do Real Damage</h3>

          <p>
            Drainage is the part of an attic install that can do real damage. A dehumidifier removing 30 or 40 pints a day is putting several gallons of water somewhere above your ceilings, and if a hose kinks, a fitting leaks or a pump fails, that water comes through the drywall. Plan the drainage before you choose the unit, and build in protection on the assumption that something will eventually fail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gravity Drainage</h3>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage is the most reliable, if you have a downhill route. Many sealed attics already contain an air handler with a condensate line running to a drain, and a dehumidifier can often be tied into that line or run its own line alongside it. Local codes govern how condensate lines are connected, often requiring a trap or air gap, so check with your HVAC installer. Keep the line sloped continuously, support it so it cannot sag, and insulate it where it passes through any unconditioned space.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Condensate Pumps</h3>

          <p>
            Where there is no downhill route, you need a pump. The AlorAir HD55P and HD35P have one built in, which is the simplest option. For the Santa Fe and AprilAire units, add an external condensate pump such as the Little Giant VCMA-15ULS, or Santa Fe's own pump accessory. Pump water to a drain, a laundry sink or an exterior discharge point where freezing and splash-back are not a problem, and remember that pumps are the component most likely to fail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Secondary Pan and Float Switch</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, put a secondary drain pan under the dehumidifier and pump, with a float switch that cuts power if water collects. This is standard practice for air handlers in attics, and the same logic applies. The Little Giant's built-in safety switch can do the same job for the pump itself. An inexpensive pan and float switch are cheap insurance against a stained ceiling or a collapsed section of drywall.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Assume the drain will fail, and plan where the water goes when it does.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Drain pan under the unit, float switch that cuts power, sloped and supported drain line, and a pump with a safety switch where gravity is not possible. An attic dehumidifier without these is a leak waiting to happen.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Hanging, Ducting and Getting It Through the Hatch
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Measure the Hatch First</h3>

          <p>
            Measure the attic hatch before anything else. The AprilAire E070 is 12.5 inches square, the Santa Fe Compact70 12 by 12, and the AlorAir units under 12 inches tall, so all of them pass through a typical 22 by 30 inch opening. The AprilAire E080 is 14 by 15 by 26 inches and 63 pounds, which fits most hatches but is awkward on a ladder. Pull-down attic stairs make this much easier; if you are replacing a hatch anyway, our <a href="/best-attic-ladder/">best attic ladder</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hanging the Unit</h3>

          <p>
            Hanging the unit keeps it off the attic floor, out of the way of storage and closer to the warm, humid air that collects at the top of a sealed attic. AlorAir sells a hanging kit with ratchet straps whose instructions explicitly cover attic installation, and Santa Fe offers a hang kit for the Compact70. Hang from framing that can carry the load, typically rafters or collar ties, and leave enough slack to lower the unit for filter changes and service.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ducting for Whole-Attic Coverage</h3>

          <p>
            Ducting lets one unit treat the whole attic rather than the corner it sits in. A short supply duct throwing dry air toward the far end of the attic, with the unit pulling from near the peak, is a common layout. Santa Fe sells separate supply and return duct kits for the Compact70, the E070 has 8-inch collars and the E080 has 10-inch collars with a backdraft damper. Every foot of duct adds static pressure and reduces airflow, so keep runs short and smooth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power and Service Access</h3>

          <p>
            Plan power and access too. Each unit here runs on a standard 115 to 120 volt outlet and draws under 6 amps, but an attic outlet should be on a circuit that is not already loaded by the air handler, and installed by an electrician if one does not exist. Leave clear access to the filter, the drain connection and the controls. A unit you cannot reach is a unit whose filter will never be cleaned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Alternatives: Supply Air, HVAC Settings and Whole-House Units
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Supply Air From Existing Ductwork</h3>

          <p>
            Before buying a dedicated attic dehumidifier, ask your HVAC contractor about supply air. A small supply register from the existing ductwork, sized at roughly 50 CFM per 1,000 square feet of attic as Building Science Corporation suggests, lets the air conditioner dry the attic whenever it runs. In a hot, humid climate where the AC runs most of the year, that is often enough on its own, and it costs far less than a dehumidifier to install and nothing extra to run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Limits of Supply Air</h3>

          <p>
            Supply air has limits. In spring and fall the air conditioner may barely run, so the attic gets little drying exactly when outdoor humidity is high and temperatures are mild. An oversized air conditioner that cycles briefly also removes little moisture. Those are the conditions where a dedicated dehumidifier earns its keep, often running only for a few months a year and sitting idle the rest.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-House Dehumidifiers</h3>

          <p>
            If the whole house feels clammy, not just the attic, a whole-house dehumidifier ducted into the HVAC system may be the better investment. The AprilAire E080 can serve that role when installed in a sealed attic with the air handler. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-dehumidifier/">whole-house dehumidifier guide</a> covers larger units and how they tie into the ductwork.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fixes for Vented Attics</h3>

          <p>
            And if the attic is vented, the alternatives are the fixes in the second section of this guide: air seal the ceiling, vent every exhaust outside, clear the soffits and use the right ventilation. Those cost less than any dehumidifier, and they solve the problem rather than managing a symptom. A dehumidifier in a vented attic is money spent drying the outdoors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a dehumidifier in my attic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not, if the attic is vented. A vented attic's moisture problems come from air leaks, bathroom fans venting into the attic or blocked soffits, and fixing those is the answer. A sealed or spray foam attic is different: if a hygrometer shows humidity staying above about 60% in summer, a compact dehumidifier or HVAC supply air is often needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dehumidifier for an attic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a sealed attic, the Santa Fe Compact70 is our top pick for its small, hangable cabinet, duct kits and published 70°F output. Choose the AlorAir HD55P if you have no downhill drain route, the HD35P for a small attic, and the AprilAire E080 if you want to duct it into an air handler in the attic.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my spray foam attic so humid?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A sealed attic is part of the house but often gets no conditioned air. Moisture from the living space, leaky ducts and vapor moving through open-cell foam builds up with nowhere to go. Building Science Corporation recommends either supply air at about 50 CFM per 1,000 square feet of attic or dehumidification to keep it in check.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a sealed attic be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim to keep a sealed attic at or below about 60% relative humidity, and ideally close to the 45% to 55% you keep in the living space. Readings consistently above 60%, condensation on ducts or a musty smell mean the attic needs supply air, dehumidification or a search for air leaks to the outside.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a portable dehumidifier in my attic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard portable is a poor choice. Its bucket fills quickly, most have no pump, few can be ducted or hung, and none is designed to run unattended above a finished ceiling. Purpose-built crawl space and whole-house units are designed for unattended operation, ducting, continuous drainage and the higher temperatures a sealed attic can reach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where does the water go from an attic dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Into a drain line, either by gravity to an existing condensate line or drain, or by pump to a drain, sink or exterior outlet. Units like the AlorAir HD55P include a pump; others need an external condensate pump. Always add a secondary drain pan with a float switch under the unit to protect the ceiling below.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will an attic fan reduce attic humidity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a vented attic, an attic fan moves air but can make moisture worse by depressurizing the attic and pulling humid, conditioned air up through ceiling leaks. Air sealing and passive soffit and ridge vents are usually more effective. In a sealed attic, an attic fan should never be used, because it breaks the seal the design depends on.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a dehumidifier in a vented attic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A vented attic exchanges air with the outdoors, so a dehumidifier there is trying to dry the weather, and summer temperatures can exceed its rated maximum. Damp sheathing or winter frost in a vented attic points to air leaks, unvented exhaust fans or blocked soffits. Fix those first; a dehumidifier is the wrong tool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the maximum operating temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Compressor dehumidifiers have a heat limit, from 95°F for the Santa Fe Compact70 to 110°F for the AlorAir HD35P. Log the attic's summer temperature before buying. A sealed attic tied to the house usually stays in range, but an unsealed or thinly foamed one may not, and the unit will shut down when you need it most.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the drain pan and safety switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An attic dehumidifier sits above finished ceilings, so a kinked hose or failed pump sends water through the drywall. Install a secondary drain pan with a float switch that cuts power, slope and support the drain line, and use a pump with an overflow safety switch where gravity drainage is not possible.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing for a sealed attic</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A properly sealed attic gains moisture slowly, so a large basement-sized unit short-cycles, wasting energy and wearing the compressor. Most attics under 1,000 square feet need about 35 pints at AHAM conditions. If a smaller unit runs constantly and humidity stays high, find the air leak before buying a bigger machine.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before trying supply air</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                In many sealed attics, a small supply register from the existing ductwork, sized around 50 CFM per 1,000 square feet, dries the attic whenever the air conditioner runs. Ask your HVAC contractor first. Add a dehumidifier if humidity still climbs above 60% in spring, fall or on mild, humid days.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

