        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most attics do not need a dehumidifier; they need air sealing, exhaust fans that vent outside and clear soffit vents. A sealed or spray foam attic is the exception. If a hygrometer shows it staying above about 60% in summer, and supply air from the HVAC system is not enough, a compact dehumidifier is the right fix. The Santa Fe Compact70 is our first choice, the AlorAir HD55P the easiest install where there is no downhill drain, and the HD35P the right size for small attics.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you choose, treat the drainage as the critical part: a pump with a safety switch where gravity will not work, a drain pan with a float switch under the unit, and a line that cannot sag or kink. For dehumidifiers in the rest of the house, start with our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-home/">best dehumidifiers for the home</a>.
